# fastbamqc

Fast mapping and depth metrics (percentage mapped, mean depth, percentage of positions covered at >= X)
for coordinate-sorted and indexed BAM files. Works on whole genomes and on amplicon / panel data.

## Installation
    pip install .            # or: pip install -e .  (development: code changes are picked up immediately)
    pip install ".[xlsx]"    # + Excel export

`pysam` is not available on native Windows: use WSL.

## Usage
Whole genome (one row per sample):

    fastbamqc -i ./bams -f "*_sorted.bam" -o ./results -F tsv -c 16

Amplicon-seq / panel (one row per gene and per sample):

    fastbamqc -i ./bams -f "*_sorted.bam" -o ./results --bed amplicons.bed -d 1,10,30,100,500

`fastbamqc --help` lists all options. BAM files must be sorted and indexed (`samtools index`).

### CPU cores
`-c N` is the total number of cores to use (default 4). In whole-genome mode a BAM is cut into independent
windows that are shared between processes, so **one big file also uses several cores**; the totals are exactly
the same whatever the number of cores. By default (`-t` not given) the cores are shared between the BAM
files: 1 file -> all the cores, 3 files on 4 cores -> 3 files at the same time. `-t N` fixes the number of
cores used by each file (files in parallel = `-c // -t`). In amplicon mode `-t` is the number of
decompression threads per file. Memory grows with the number of processes (about 100-250 MiB each on the
test data).

## Interpretability (on by default)
Every result row (one sample in whole-genome mode, one gene in amplicon mode) gets an extra last column
`interpretability`. By default the rule is **at least 75% of the region covered at >= 10X**:

    fastbamqc -i ./bams -o ./results --bed amplicons.bed                                  # 75% at 10X
    fastbamqc -i ./bams -o ./results --bed amplicons.bed --interp-depth 20 --interp-pct 90
    fastbamqc -i ./bams -o ./results --no-interp                                          # no column, no sliders

A row is `INTERPRETABLE` when at least `--interp-pct` percent of its region (the genome, or the gene) is covered by
at least `--interp-depth` reads, and `NON_INTERPRETABLE` otherwise.

- `--interp-depth` and `--interp-pct` are independent: the one you omit keeps its default (10X / 75%).
- `--interp-nodup` (whole genome only): by default the rule uses the depth of **all** reads. With this option
  duplicate reads (flagged 0x400 **or** estimated from the alignments, see `--read-stats`) are left out. The
  table gets the columns `<d>X_nodup` (breadth of coverage without duplicates, one per depth threshold) and the
  rule reads `<interp-depth>X_nodup`. Recommended for libraries with many duplicates (WGA, low input), where
  the raw depth overstates the usable depth. Cost: every read is checked for duplication (about +50-60% run
  time on synthetic data; not measured on real BAMs). Not available with `--bed` or `--no-interp`. In the
  report, the sliders, breadth-of-coverage charts, depth distribution and coverage curves then describe the
  depth without duplicates (per-contig values still include them) and the CSV file name ends with `_nodup`.
- `--no-interp` removes the column (and the report sliders); it cannot be combined with the two options above.
- The depth threshold is added to the `<d>X` columns if it is not already in `--depths`, so the verdict can always be
  re-checked from the table.
- The decision uses the 2-decimal value shown in the table (`>=` comparison: exactly 75.00 is INTERPRETABLE).
- In whole-genome mode the region is the set of analysed contigs: use `--contigs` to exclude decoy/alt contigs.

## HTML report (optional)
Add `--report` to get a single self-contained HTML file (`<out-dir>/<out-name>.html`) next to the tables.
It has no external dependency: it opens offline in any browser and can be e-mailed.

    fastbamqc -i ./bams -o ./results --bed amplicons.bed --report --report-title "Run 42"

- **Overview** tab: summary cards, comparison charts of all samples and the full sortable/filterable table (CSV download).
  - whole genome: mean coverage per sample (coloured by interpretability), % mapped, breadth of coverage, a **Duplicates** section, coverage curves;
  - amplicon: targets x samples heatmap (choose the metric), interpretable targets per sample, coverage and interpretability rate per target.
- **Sample details** tab: pick a sample in the menu (or use the arrows).
  - whole genome: metrics, breadth of coverage, duplicates and read breakdown, depth distribution, coverage curve, mean depth per contig (largest 100 contigs);
  - amplicon: table of the targets, any metric per target, depth profile along each target (selector, shared y-axis, log scale) and a gallery of all targets.
- `--report` makes the analysis slightly slower (depth histograms/profiles are collected); the tables are identical with or without it.
- In whole-genome mode `--report` turns `--read-stats` on (needed for the Duplicates section; about +50-60% run time on synthetic data, see below). The section is not shown in amplicon mode, where duplicates are expected.
- A link such as `report.html#sample=S1` opens directly on a sample.


**Live interpretability rule.** The report always has two
sliders in the header (unless `--no-interp`) (depth X and minimum % covered) re-evaluate every result live: verdicts, charts,
tables and the **CSV download** all follow the sliders (the file name carries the values, e.g.
`fastbamqc_all_results_10X_75pct.csv`; the `<X>X` column of the chosen depth is added when it is not in
`--depths`). The percentage covered at each slider depth is computed exactly by fastbamqc (52 depths:
1-20, then 25-100 by 5, 120-300 by 20, 350, 400, 500, 600, 800, 1000, plus your `--depths`), so the
verdicts are identical to a new run with the same thresholds. The report grows by about 0.3 KB per
table row (roughly 3 MB for 96 samples x 100 targets; not measured at that size). With `--no-interp` there is no rule and no sliders.

**Coverage curves.** The x axis of the coverage curves stops at 200X, or at your largest `--depths`
value when it is higher (and earlier when the data stops before).

## Extra metrics (optional)

Both options are off by default: without them the output and the speed are unchanged.

### `--uniformity` (whole genome and amplicon)

| column | meaning |
|---|---|
| `median_coverage` | median depth |
| `cv_coverage` | standard deviation / mean depth (0 = perfectly even) |
| `pct_above_0.2_mean` | % of positions with depth >= 0.2 x the mean (classic uniformity) |
| `fold_80` | mean depth / depth reached by 80% of the positions (1 = even; same idea as Picard's `FOLD_80_BASE_PENALTY`) |

In amplicon mode they are computed per target (exact). In whole-genome mode they come from a depth
histogram capped at 1000X: a value that would need the capped tail is reported as `NA`. Cost: negligible.

### `--read-stats` (whole genome only)

| column | meaning |
|---|---|
| `pct_properly_paired`, `pct_mapq0` | % of primary mapped reads |
| `primary_mapped_reads` | primary mapped reads (denominator of the percentages) |
| `secondary_reads`, `supplementary_reads`, `qcfail_reads` | counts |
| `marked_dup_reads`, `pct_marked_dup` | reads flagged 0x400 in the BAM |
| `est_dup_reads`, `pct_est_dup` | duplicates **estimated from the alignments** (see below) |
| `mean_coverage_nodup` | mean depth without the duplicates (flagged **or** estimated) |

These counters ignore `--min-mapq` / `--exclude-flags` (only `mean_coverage_nodup` follows them) and
cover the selected contigs. Percentages use the primary mapped reads as denominator.

**How duplicates are estimated.** A BAM that was never run through `MarkDuplicates` has no 0x400 flag.
In a coordinate-sorted BAM the reads that start at the same position are consecutive, so a primary read
is counted as a duplicate when an earlier read has the same start, end, strand, mate start and template
length (a duplicated pair counts as 2 reads). Only the keys of the current start position are kept in
memory. This is an **estimate**, not Picard / samtools markdup:
- reads that differ only by soft clipping are not matched (Picard uses the unclipped 5' position);
- at very high depth on a small genome, independent fragments can coincide by chance and are counted
  as duplicates (0.01% on a synthetic 15X genome; not measured at 250X). When your BAMs have been
  marked, compare `pct_est_dup` with `pct_marked_dup` to see how close the estimate is on your data.

**Cost.** `--read-stats` adds a per-read check: on a synthetic 800,000-read BAM the run took about
+50-60% (1.0 s -> 1.6 s). `--uniformity` alone: no measurable change.

The HTML report adds the matching charts (duplicate rate, mean coverage with and without duplicates,
evenness) when these columns are present.

## Amplicon / panel mode (`--bed`)
BED columns: 1 chromosome, 2 start (0-based), 3 end (exclusive), 4 gene name.
Without a 4th column, the target is named `chrom:start-end`.

Outputs:
- `<out-dir>/<out-name>.<fmt>`: all rows of all samples (concatenated);
- `<out-dir>/per_sample/<sample>.amplicons.<fmt>`: one file per sample.

Columns: `sample, gene, chrom, start, end, length, n_regions, reads, percentage_of_mapped, mean_coverage, <d>X`.

BED lines sharing the same name (`--same-gene`):
- `merge` (default): grouped into ONE target; their regions are merged (no double counting).
  `start`/`end` = extent of the target, `length` = bases actually targeted, `n_regions` = number of merged regions;
- `separate`: every BED line is an independent target (the name may repeat).

Two different names are never merged, even if they overlap: each gene is computed independently.

Notes:
- BED contig names must match the BAM ones (`chr1` vs `1`), otherwise an explicit error is raised.
- Do NOT exclude duplicates (`--exclude-flags 1024`) on amplicon data: almost all reads are "duplicates" by construction.
